{
 "_doc": "The measured paint items (crosswalk bars, lines, stop bars) are drawn as a mask on a STEP_M grid in the survey's frame; the lidar's ground returns (class 2) in the same tile are moved over it in STEP_M steps up to MAX_M each way, and at each shift the mean log-intensity of the returns that land on paint is taken. Paint returns more of the laser than asphalt, so the shift where that mean peaks is where the lidar puts the paint. The lidar is another sensor flown two years earlier (2020-05-27) and registered on its own, so the peak shift is the aerial's position error against it (plus paint moved between the two dates, which is rare and local). A tile is reported only with at least MIN_RETURNS on paint at the peak and a peak at least MIN_CONTRAST over the zero-shift mean of the whole search window's median.",
 "thresholds_invented": {
  "STEP_M": 0.15,
  "MAX_M": 1.2,
  "MIN_RETURNS": 200,
  "MIN_CONTRAST": 0.3,
  "LINE_HALF_W_M": 0.08,
  "BAR_HALF_W_M": 0.25
 },
